# eldLedger Cursor Rules & Guidelines

## 1. Project Overview & Architecture
- **Project Name:** eldLedger (Self-hosted double-entry bookkeeping with intuitive cashbook UX)
- **Architecture Principle:** Strict Separation of Concerns between `Transaction` (User view) and `JournalEntry` / `JournalLine` (Accounting view).
- **Core Stacks:**
  - Backend: Python 3.11+, FastAPI, SQLAlchemy 2.0 (declarative), Pydantic v2, Alembic
  - Frontend: React 18+, TypeScript, Vite, Material UI (MUI), PWA
  - Database: SQLite (stored at `/data/database/eldledger.db`)
  - Currency: KRW (Use integer / BigInteger for all amounts, strictly NO float).

## 2. Strict Accounting & Integrity Constraints
- **Double-Entry Balance:** Every `JournalEntry` MUST satisfy `sum(debit) == sum(credit)`. Reject/raise error if unbalanced.
- **Immutability:** Confirmed transactions and journal entries must NOT be directly deleted or updated in place. Use reversals/correction entries.
- **Scope & Mixed Transactions:**
  - Support `PERSONAL`, `BUSINESS`, and `MIXED` scopes.
  - Multi-item split transactions must generate exact matching multi-line debits and credits.

## 3. UI/UX Terminology Policy
- **Primary Input Form:** NEVER expose technical accounting jargon (Debit, Credit, Journal, Ledger) to standard users.
- Use intuitive Korean terms: 수입, 지출, 이체, 개인/사업 구분, 결제수단, 분류.
- Technical terms are only displayed in the Advanced Reports section (시산표, 분개장, 총계정원장).

## 4. Coding Style
- Type hints strictly required (Python Pydantic / TypeScript Strict mode).
- Follow Clean Architecture: `routers/` -> `services/` -> `repositories/` -> `models/`.
- All core accounting rules must have unit tests verifying zero-balance invariant.